to occur.\par \pard \plain \s18 \fi-720 \li720 \sl240 \tx360 \f16 \tab Will auto turn-off when in foreground (uses the time-out setting from system screen)\par \pard \plain \s18 \fi-720 \li720 \sl240 \tx360 \f16 \tab Any Wve file may be used as alarm sound\par \pard \plain \s18 \fi-720 \li720 \sl240 \tx360 \f16 \tab Plays \plain \f16 \b any\plain \f16  alarm sound for up to 45 seconds (regardless of how long sound is)\par \pard \plain \s18 \fi-720 \li720 \sl240 \tx360 \f16 \tab Stops alarm playback after 45 seconds, repeats alarm every 5 minutes up to 4 times\par \pard \plain \s18 \fi-720 \li720 \sl240 \tx360 \f16 \tab Saves prefs (if desired)\par \pard \plain \s18 \fi-720 \li720 \sl240 \tx360 \f16 \tab Customizable snooze setting\par \pard \plain \s18 \fi-720 \li720 \sl240 \tx360 \f16 \tab Psion still useable while alarm is snoozed\par \pard \plain \s18 \fi-720 \li720 \sl240 \tx360 \f16 \tab Easily turn off Psion - just hit the space bar\par \pard \plain \s18 \fi-720 \li720 \sl240 \tx360 \f16 *\tab Detects if 12 or 24 hour time format is in use, and displays accordingly\par \pard \plain \s18 \fi-720 \li720 \sl240 \tx360 \f16 *\tab Esc key sends it to the background (last application comes to foreground)\par \pard \plain \s18 \fi-720 \li720 \sl240 \tx360 \f16 *\tab Display shows if an alarm is pending, and when it will ring.\par \pard \plain \s18 \fi-720 \li720 \sl240 \tx360 \f16 *\tab Option to come to foreground when machine is idle (\plain \f16 \b only\plain \f16  when auto-off inactive)\par \pard \plain \s18 \fi-720 \li720 \sl240 \tx360 \f16 \tab Proper monitoring of system events, will quit if the system asks it to\par \pard \plain \s18 \fi-720 \li720 \sl240 \tx360 \f16 \tab \tab (even when in foreground, to keep rcom users happy)\par \pard \plain \s18 \fi-720 \li720 \sl240 \tx360 \f16 \tab "Nifty" shadowed numerals\par \pard \plain \s18 \fi-720 \li720 \sl240 \tx360 \f16 \par \pard \plain \s18 \fi-720 \li720 \sl240 \tx360 \f16 * New/improved feature\par \pard \plain \s18 \fi-720 \li720 \sl240 \tx360 \f16 \par \pard \plain \s19 \sl240 \f16 Its use is pretty straightforward. The clock runs continuously, if you set an alarm it will ring when next that time of day occurs (ie if it is 8am, an alarm set for 7am will ring the next day, whereas an alarm set for 10 am will ring later that same day). The space bar turns the psion off immediately. To snooze a ringing alarm, hit the space bar, any other key will silence the alarm. (The Psion can be configured turn off when snoozed, if desired.) The program is about 8k on disk, and uses 9-10k of memory for itself and the system uses 17-18k additional while it is running. (I've observed this to go to 26k while an alarm is sounding). These values are on my 512k 3a, which is the only machine this has been tested on.\par \pard \plain \s19 \sl240 \f16 \par \pard \plain \s19 \sl240 \f16 This program is "cheap shareware" - if you use it, do something nice for some one that you wouldn't have done anyway. (Kind of pink and squishy sounding, I admit.) Personally I'm fed up with overpriced shareware for the Psion; IMHO, anyone charging more than 10-15$ for a program should have a very good reason why it's worth it, and the limited size of the potential market is \plain \f16 \b not\plain \f16  such a reason. I'd rather people use my work and enjoy it.  Sharing programs you have written for the Psion without charging for them would qualify as "something nice", especially if you share them with me!\par \pard \plain \s19 \sl240 \f16 \par \pard \plain \s19 \sl240 \f16 Note: this program does \plain \f16 \b not\plain \f16  work if run on the PC Psion emulator. It panics when the call to the FRC: driver is made; I assume this driver must not exist in the emulator environment.\par \pard \plain \s19 \sl240 \f16 \par \pard \plain \s19 \sl240 \f16 I've tried to make this program as complete as possible - the only other thing I thought of was multiple alarms. I've figured out how I would do that, but it would take a lot of work and time so I think it may not happen. (I'm a 4th year Medical student, so I'm not supposed to spend all my time writing code!) I'd also like to rewrite the code to be asynchronous, as demonstrated in Appman (thanks Jezar), so the clock would continue to run while menus and dialogs were up, but I just don't understand the Window server well enough to tackle this (eagerly awaiting the release of the Wserver psionics file...but begining to lose hope...) \par \pard \plain \s19 \sl240 \f16 \par \pard \plain \s19 \sl240 \f16 If you can think of any other functions that would be good, or just wish to give me feedback, please contact me. (note: I mean this. I've implemented all the requests made so far, so if you want something, don't hesitate to ask.)\par \pard \plain \s19 \sl240 \f16 \par \pard \plain \s19 \sl240 \f16 The normal legal stuff applies - although I've done my best to make sure this program won't hurt you, use it at your own risk, there are no warrantees. I retain all rights to this program and the code, if you want the code email me and I'm likely to be agreeable (and it will be a lot more understandable than Revtran generated!). If you wish to put this on any media which you are selling for profit (CD or floppy) please contact me for permission.\par \pard \plain \s19 \sl240 \f16 \par \pard \plain \s19 \sl240 \f16 All of the functions are pretty self-evident, however for those who *like* to read these readme files...\par \pard \plain \s19 \sl240 \f16 \par \pard \plain \s18 \fi-720 \li720 \sl240 \tx360 \f16 \b MENU\plain \f16  choices\par \pard \plain \s18 \fi-720 \li720 \sl240 \tx360 \f16 \par \pard \plain \s18 \fi-720 \li720 \sl240 \tx360 \f16 \tab \plain \f16 \b Set\plain \f16  \plain \f16 \b Alarm\plain \f16  (psion-s)\par \pard \plain \s18 \fi-720 \li720 \sl240 \tx360 \f16 \par \pard \plain \s20 \li1440 \sl240 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \f16 Allows you to set an alarm. The default time and mode (once vs. daily) are stored in the prefs file if it exists, otherwise the defaults of 8:00 am and once are offered)\par \pard \plain \s18 \fi-720 \li720 \sl240 \tx360 \f16 \par \pard \plain \s18 \fi-720 \li720 \sl240 \tx360 \f16 \tab \plain \f16 \b Preferences\plain \f16  (psion-p)\par \pard \plain \s18 \fi-720 \li720 \sl240 \tx360 \f16 \par \pard \plain \s20 \li1440 \sl240 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \f16 Allows you to create a preferences file that will be used for default settings. The default settings (without a prefs file) are:\par \pard \plain \s20 \li1440 \sl240 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \f16 \par \pard \plain \s20 \li1440 \sl240 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \f16 Default Alarm\tab Chimes\par \pard \plain \s20 \li1440 \sl240 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \f16 Default Time\tab \tab 8:00 am\par \pard \plain \s20 \li1440 \sl240 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \f16 Snooze Time\tab \tab 7 minutes\par \pard \plain \s20 \li1440 \sl240 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \f16 Alarm Volume\tab Loud\par \pard \plain \s20 \li1440 \sl240 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \f16 Daily Alarm\tab \tab No\par \pard \plain \s20 \li1440 \sl240 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \f16 Off on Snooze\tab Yes\par \pard \plain \s20 \li1440 \sl240 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \f16 Front on Idle\tab \tab No\par \pard \plain \s20 \li1440 \sl240 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \f16 Prefs Disk\tab \tab M (Internal)\par \pard \plain \s20 \li1440 \sl240 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \f16 \par \pard \plain \s21 \fi-360 \li1800 \sl240 \f16 Alarm - may be any sound in a WVE directory, or the three sounds in ROM (Chimes, Fanfare, Soft bells).\par \pard \plain \s21 \fi-360 \li1800 \sl240 \f16 \par \pard \plain \s21 \fi-360 \li1800 \sl240 \f16 Time - any time\par \pard \plain \s21 \fi-360 \li1800 \sl240 \f16 \par \pard \plain \s21 \fi-360 \li1800 \sl240 \f16 Snooze Time - from 5 to 15 minutes\par \pard \plain \s21 \fi-360 \li1800 \sl240 \f16 \par \pard \plain \s21 \fi-360 \li1800 \sl240 \f16 Alarm Volume - Soft, Medium, Loud, Ouch!\par \pard \plain \s21 \fi-360 \li1800 \sl240 \f16 \par \pard \plain \s21 \fi-360 \li1800 \sl240 \f16 Daily Alarm - Yes or No (note even if this is Yes, you still need to set the alarm after you load the program, it will NOT "turn on" the alarm without being told to)\par \pard \plain \s21 \fi-360 \li1800 \sl240 \f16 \par \pard \plain \s21 \fi-360 \li1800 \sl240 \f16 Off on Snooze - turn the Psion off when an alarm is snoozed.\par \pard \plain \s21 \fi-360 \li1800 \sl240 \f16 \par \pard \plain \s21 \fi-360 \li1800 \sl240 \f16 Front on Idle - OK, I thought this was a strange request at first, but the idea kind of grew on me with time...like a fungus or a tick. Basically, after the machine is idle for a period of time, the clock will come to the foreground. I've taken \plain \f16 \b great\plain \f16  pains to make sure this only happens when auto-off is disabled, either by being turned off, or by being temporarily disabled since the machine is on external power. The idle period is either the off time + approx. 10 secs, or 5 minutes if auto-off is completely disabled (since there is no off time under these circumstances)\par \pard \plain \s21 \fi-360 \li1800 \sl240 \f16 \par \pard \plain \s21 \fi-360 \li1800 \sl240 \f16 Prefs Disk - M, A, or B.  \plain \f16 \b Note:\plain \f16  this is the order the disks are searched for a prefs file at startup. If you change where this file is stored, only the first prefs file found will be used.  It is located in the OPD directory.\par \pard \plain \s20 \li1440 \sl240 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \f16 \par \pard \plain \s18 \fi-720 \li720 \sl240 \tx360 \f16 \tab \plain \f16 \b Cancel Alarm\plain \f16  (psion-c)\par \pard \plain \s18 \fi-720 \li720 \sl240 \tx360 \f16 \par \pard \plain \s20 \li1440 \sl240 \tx2880 \tx3600 \tx4320 \f16 Cancels the pending alarm.  If the alarm is set to daily, it will prompt to cancel just the next alarm, or to turn off the daily alarms.\pa